1
00:00:51,196 --> 00:00:59,526
The planner starts on the Data page. The 'Synthetic data' badge is on every screen:
Company A's data is not available.

2
00:00:59,576 --> 00:01:05,098
The planner drops in this week's files: eight CSV files in one ZIP.

3
00:01:05,148 --> 00:01:15,950
Every file is checked before anything is calculated. Valid: 2 suppliers, 3 warehouses,
6 zones, 20 products, 606 pallets.

4
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,450
What if the data is wrong? This file declares a loop between warehouses.

5
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:32,950
The run is blocked and the message says exactly why. There is no Calculate button: the
system never guesses.

6
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:42,417
Back to the valid data set, ready to calculate.

7
00:01:42,467 --> 00:01:56,293
The optimiser finds a plan costing 31,316 cost units against 32,698 for the baseline:
a saving of 1,382 (4.2%).

8
00:01:56,343 --> 00:02:05,297
This is synthetic data, so it is not a forecast. The baseline is a simple
preferred-warehouse rule, not Company A's real plan.

9
00:02:05,347 --> 00:02:13,773
Plan checks passed: a separate verifier, which does not use the solver, re-checked
supply, demand, capacity and lanes.

10
00:02:13,823 --> 00:02:21,963
The diagram shows where goods move: coloured lines carry pallets, grey dashed lines
are declared routes left unused.

11
00:02:22,013 --> 00:02:28,710
The ranking prices every full path, leg by leg, so the planner can see why a route is
chosen.

12
00:02:28,760 --> 00:02:38,026
For Zone 1 cement the cheapest path is Supplier 1 via Warehouse C, at 62 per pallet.
Selecting a row highlights the whole path.

13
00:02:38,076 --> 00:02:45,893
Now the planner overrides the machine. A customer contract says Zone 1 must receive
its cement from Warehouse B.

14
00:02:45,943 --> 00:02:53,910
The planner fixes 8 pallets of Cement standard to Warehouse B and must give a reason.

15
00:02:53,960 --> 00:03:01,047
Everything else is recalculated around that decision as a new plan version.

16
00:03:01,097 --> 00:03:14,899
Result: the plan cost goes from 31,316 to 31,404, plus 88 cost units. The price of the
decision is visible and the original plan is kept.

17
00:03:14,949 --> 00:03:22,967
Now the explanation. Code calculates every number; the AI assistant only puts the
result into words.

18
00:03:23,017 --> 00:03:32,115
The text is marked 'AI draft, needs review'. If any figure is not in the calculated
facts, the whole draft is rejected.

19
00:03:32,165 --> 00:03:39,515
Even so, the planner must read it: the number check is one guard, not proof that every
sentence is right.

20
00:03:39,565 --> 00:03:50,415
The supporting figures let the planner compare the text with the calculated numbers
before approving.

21
00:03:50,465 --> 00:03:59,028
Here is that guard in action, outside the app. A draft with correct figures is
ACCEPTED.

22
00:03:59,078 --> 00:04:07,315
A draft that invents a figure is REJECTED, and the standard summary is used instead.

23
00:04:07,365 --> 00:04:19,215
The planner edits the text if needed and approves it. AI helps; a person decides.

24
00:04:19,265 --> 00:04:27,815
Now the planner approves the plan. From this moment it is locked, and only an approved
plan can be exported.

25
00:04:27,865 --> 00:04:36,099
The export is a ZIP for the existing ERP, warehouse and transport systems. Nordic Flow
executes nothing.

26
00:04:36,149 --> 00:04:46,087
The audit trail records every step: data loaded, plan calculated, the planner's
decision, approvals and export.

27
00:04:46,137 --> 00:04:55,499
Each entry is linked to the one before it, so any edit is detectable: tamper-evident,
not tamper-proof.

28
00:04:55,549 --> 00:05:03,999
The planner can also ask 'what if'. If Warehouse A closes, no plan can meet demand.

29
00:05:04,049 --> 00:05:14,947
Total warehouse capacity (684) is below the demand that needs handling (902). The
system says so instead of returning a bad plan.

30
00:05:14,997 --> 00:05:23,101
It lists the unserved pallets by zone and product, and never invents a route.

